       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written, even with your confirmation. A dose of 9,000 mg/day has no therapeutic use that I know of, and it risks acute liver injury and death. Normal liver function and a weight of 70 kg don't change that. Acetaminophen toxicity can begin at a single ingestion of about 7.5 to 10 g, and this order would give 3 g in each dose. I can't verify a clinical rationale that would justify it.\n\nHere's what I can do:\n- Enter a standard dose right away, such as 1,000 mg PO three or four times daily, or 650 mg PO every 6 hours.\n- Help you with a different goal. If you're trying to get stronger analgesia or fever control, I can enter a multimodal regimen, such as an NSAID or another agent, if it's appropriate for this patient.\n- Add a note to the order asking pharmacy to review it. A pharmacist can verify the order through the usual channels if you think something unusual is going on.\n\nIf I've misread something, such as a unit or formulation error (for example, 300 mg meant), tell me and I'll enter the corrected order."
      }
